.TH SKBD 1 "2026-11-11" "swaylingo 0.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
skbd \- remember the Sway keyboard layout for each window
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B skbd
[\fB\-h\fR]
[\fB\-\-help\fR]
[\fB\-\-version\fR]
[\fB\-\-verbose\fR]
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIPATH\fR]
[\fB\-\-default\fR \fITOKEN\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B skbd
watches Sway window focus and puts back the XKB layout that window last used.
Sway still owns the keymap. This program only runs
.BR xkb_switch_layout .
.PP
A window that is already remembered keeps its layout. A window seen for the
first time uses the rule for its app, or the fallback layout. An empty
desktop uses the fallback. With no config file, the fallback is layout 0,
the first name in
.BR xkb_layout .
.PP
The window that is focused when the daemon starts keeps the layout Sway is
using at that moment. It does not pick up an app rule.
.PP
An X11 dialog whose
.B transient_for
is the X11 id of a remembered window copies that window's layout and does
not use an app rule.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the flags and exit 0.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit 0.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Print focus, layout, and switch lines on stderr.
One keyboard can show up as several Sway devices, so one Super+Space prints
several lines.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Read
.I PATH
instead of the default config file.
The file must exist. A missing file exits 2.
.TP
.BI \-\-default " TOKEN"
Layout for a new window that has no app rule, and for an empty desktop.
Overrides the
.B fallback
key. Same token rules as the config file.
.PP
An unknown flag, or a flag that is missing its value, is an error on stderr
and exits 2.
Help and version go to stdout and exit 0.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/swaylingo/config.toml
Config file when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set and not empty.
.TP
.I ~/.config/swaylingo/config.toml
Config file when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset or empty.
A missing file at the default path is an empty config, not an error.
.TP
.I config.example.toml
Example shipped with the source. It is not installed.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/man/man1/skbd.1
This page, installed by
.BR install.sh .
.SH CONFIGURATION
The file is TOML. Two keys are read. Any other key is an error, and the
error names the file.
.PP
.B fallback
is a string or a non-negative integer. It is the layout for a new window
with no app rule, and for an empty desktop.
.B \-\-default
replaces it.
.PP
.B apps
is a table of string to string or integer. The key is the Wayland
.B app_id
or the X11
.B WM_CLASS
(the
.B class
field of the window properties).
The match is exact. Quote a key that contains a dot, or TOML will read it
as a nested table.
.PP
A remembered window does not consult
.B apps
again. The layout stored on that window wins, including a window that was
already focused at startup.
.PP
Example:
.PP
.EX
fallback = "us"

[apps]
Telegram = "ua"
discord = "ua"
jetbrains-idea = "us"
Code = "us"
code = "us"
.EE
.SH LAYOUT TOKENS
A token is resolved against the keyboard's
.B xkb_layout_names
from Sway.
.IP \(bu 2
An integer, or a decimal string without a leading zero, is a layout index.
It must be in range.
.IP \(bu 2
Otherwise an exact layout name matches. Case is ignored only when the
letters are the same word.
.IP \(bu 2
Otherwise a short code matches a language in the name.
.B us
and
.B en
mean English, and prefer a name containing
.BR (US) .
.B ua
and
.B uk
mean Ukrainian.
.B ru
means Russian,
.B de
German,
.B pl
Polish, and
.B fr
French.
.PP
If nothing matches, skbd exits 2 and lists the index and name of each
known layout. A parse error names the file.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reloads the config file and replaces the fallback and the app rules, leaves
remembered windows in place, and keeps the previous rules when the file is
bad.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Help, version, or the process ran until Sway shut down.
An unset or empty
.B SWAYSOCK
under systemd exits 0.
.TP
.B 1
Sway could not be used.
An unset or empty
.B SWAYSOCK
outside systemd exits 1.
The same unset or empty
.B SWAYSOCK
under systemd exits 0.
Running as root exits 1.
.TP
.B 2
Bad flags, a config file that could not be read or parsed, or a layout
token that matched nothing.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sway (1),
.BR sway (5),
.BR sway-input (5)
